The two tubes have to be at least 32 km apart to keep the mirrors from hitt=
ing=20
each other, and that's if they're never quite fully open and any maneuvers=
=20
involving spinning the two tubes at different speeds are scheduled for time=
s=20
when the mirrors are at least 2/3 closed. (When the mirrors are fully open,=
=20
their tips will be accelerating at 81g, or 794 m/s=B2. How we'll keep them =
from=20
flinging off into space, I don't know.) This means there's a corridor at=20
least 32 km long, possibly 64, at each end of the tubes. Vehicles of some=
=20
sort travel from one tube to the other, carrying stuff and people between t=
he=20
two tubes. The docking station is in the middle of one of these corridors,=
=20
the one closer to the Sun, I think. So when Alpha and Beta arrive, they tak=
e=20
one of these corridor cars to the tube, and go down the elevator to Beta's=
=20
town.

Pierre
